-- title:
--   n-torsion of an abelian scheme is defined over a finite subextension
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, $A$ a scheme, and $f : A \to \operatorname{Spec} K$ a morphism. Let $L$ be a relative group law for $f$: for every test scheme $T$ with structure morphism $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} K$ it equips the set of $T$-points of $A$ over $t$, i.e. $\{\varphi : T \to A \mid \varphi \circ f = t\}$, with a multiplication, unit and inverse satisfying associativity, the unit laws and left inversion, compatibly with precomposition along morphisms $\psi : T' \to T$ over $\operatorname{Spec} K$; assume $L$ is commutative ($hc$). Assume the bundle $hA$: $f$ is smooth and proper, each fibre $f^{-1}(s)$ over a point $s$ of $\operatorname{Spec} K$ is connected, and $f$ admits some relative group law. Let $g : \mathbb{N}$ be such that every such fibre has topological Krull dimension $g$. Let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field which is an algebraic extension of $K$, and $n : \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ne 0$ in $K$. Then there is an intermediate field $F$ of $\Omega/K$, finite-dimensional over $K$, such that: first, every $P$ in the $n$-torsion of the additive group $A(\Omega) =$ `L.AlgPoints hc Ω` of $\Omega$-points of $A$ over $K$ (those $P$ with $n \cdot P = 0$) is the base change of an $F$-point, i.e. there is $P' : \operatorname{Spec} F \to A$ with $P' \circ f$ the structure morphism $\operatorname{Spec} F \to \operatorname{Spec} K$ and with $P'$ precomposed with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $F \to \Omega$ equal to the morphism underlying $P$; and second, every $K$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\Omega$ fixing $F$ pointwise satisfies $\sigma \bullet P = P$ for every $n$-torsion point $P$.
--
--   This is the finiteness of the field $K(A[n])$ generated by the $n$-torsion of an abelian variety, in the form that the $n$-torsion points are all rational over one finite subextension $F \subseteq \Omega$ and are therefore fixed by the automorphisms of $\Omega/K$ trivial on $F$; it is what makes the Galois action on torsion, and hence on Tate modules, continuous level by level. It rests on the count $\#A(\Omega)[n] = n^{2g}$ together with a spreading-out result descending a morphism of base changes to a finite subextension, and it is used in the construction of the quaternionic Čerednik–Drinfeld data.
